.docname {Profiling and benchmarks}
.include {docs}

## Baseline

```shell
cargo run --release
cargo test --all-targets --all-features --locked
cargo bench --locked
```

Profile release builds only. Dev builds optimize this crate lightly, so their timings mislead.

## Benchmarks

Benchmarks use [Criterion](https://bheisler.github.io/criterion.rs/book/) and live in `benches/`.

### board_step

```shell
cargo bench --bench board_step     # or: just bench, which runs both benchmarks
```

Times a step together with taking the cells that changed, which is what each frame does:

- **glider:** a glider on 256, 512, and 1024 boards, where only a few rows are evaluated
- **noise:** deterministic random fills at 30% and 45% density on the same sizes, stepped on and on, so they settle into debris
- **dense:** a freshly seeded 512, 1024, and 2048 board on every iteration, so every row is evaluated every time
- **sparse board:** the glider and the fresh 30% noise on `SparseBoard`, with its dirty chunks extracted, to compare the two boards directly
- **debris:** one generation of a board settled for 2000 generations, from the same state each time

### frame_loop

```shell
cargo bench --bench frame_loop
```

Times generations at ∞ the way the app's frames run them, on a 1024 × 1024 board with 25% noise, randomized again every 2000 generations, with cell age off and on. Each frame steps for the max-speed budget and paints the board's tiles, through the same systems the app uses (`render_plugin::frame_loop` builds them without a window). A frame then waits for the next refresh of a 120 Hz display if it finished early, as frames on a variable-refresh display do. Criterion reports the time per generation, waits included, so it is the inverse of generations per second at ∞.

`board_step` catches a slower step; this catches fewer steps per frame, from a smaller frame budget or slower painting, which no step benchmark sees. Run it before and after any change to `simulation_plugin.rs`, the max-speed budget, or how the board is drawn. It leaves out the window, UI, and GPU upload, so the app's real frames are a little longer and its rate a little lower.

## CPU profiling

- **macOS:** run `cargo run --release` and attach the Time Profiler in Instruments.
- **Linux:** use `perf`, or `cargo flamegraph` if installed.

## In-app numbers

Press .keybinding {F3} for the [diagnostics view](interface.qd#diagnostics). It shows the average step time, the tick interval, and the measured rate in generations per second, which is the quickest check that a change helped on a real board.
